ute"] ", who had twice equaled the world record of 21.71, once in winning the European title; and " [:a {:href "/athletes/69661"} "Silke Gladisch"] ", the 1987 World Champion. America’s top bet was " [:a {:href "/athletes/77849"} "Florence Griffith Joyner"] ", second in this event 1984, but with the East Germans absent. She had been dominant at the US Olympic Trials, running 21.77 in the semi-finals for an American record, and shattering the 100 world record in 10.49. In Seoul Griffith Joyner had already won the 100 by over three metres. In the 200 she broke the US and Olympic record in the quarter-finals with 21.76, and then in the semi-finals, posted a world record 21.56. The final was highly anticipated but it was not much of a race as FloJo destroyed the field, winning by four metres from Jamaica’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/72218"} "Grace Jackson "] " with Drechsler another two metres back in third. Her world record time of 21.34 was considered Beamonesque and still stood as the world record 20 years later."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "R1"] [:th {} "QF"] [:th {} "SF"] [:th {} "Final"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "569"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/77849"} "Florence Griffith Joyner"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/USA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/USA.png"}] "USA"]] [:td {} "22.51 (1 h5)"] [:td {} "21.76 (1 h1)"] [:td {} "21.56 (1 h1)"] [:td {} "21.34 (1)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {} [:span {:class "label label-default"} [:abbrev {:title "World Record"} "WR"]]] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {:class "bib"} "365"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/72218"} "Grace Jackson"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/JAM"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; ver